    id: "CTW_0270",
    topic: "Watt vs. Newcomen: The Evolution of the Steam Engine",
    text: "In 1765, James Watt, a Scottish instrument maker, repaired a Newcomen model and saw the flaw. He invented the \"Separate Condenser.\" Watt added a separate chamber connected to the main cylinder. The steam was sucked into this cold chamber to condense. This meant the main cylinder could remain hot *all the time*. This simple change improved fuel efficiency by 75%. Suddenly, steam engines were cheap enough to be used not just in coal mines (where fuel was free), but in factories, textile mills, and eventually, locomotives.",
